Crash-proof relay demo
======================

Demonstrates that a stateful relay server (tcpr-bridge) can CRASH and RECOVER
mid-transfer without either client (plain `nc`, no TCPR awareness) noticing,
and proves no data was lost with an end-to-end checksum.

Topology (see ../recover for the same style):

    [ peer netns 192.0.2.2 ]  <--bridge-->  [ application netns 192.0.2.1 ]
      nc sender / receiver        TCPR           tcpr-bridge (recoverable)

Prerequisites: the userspace tools and kernel module are built and installed
(see ../../README), and you are root.

Steps (use several terminals):

  # terminal 0 (host)
  ./setup-network
  ./setup-tcpr
  ./make-payload 200M        # writes /tmp/in.dat and prints its sha256

  # terminal 1 - the recoverable relay
  ./server

  # terminal 2 - receiver client (connect FIRST)
  ./recv

  # terminal 3 - sender client
  ./send

  # ---- MID-TRANSFER: kill the relay in terminal 1 (^C), then: ----
  ./recover                  # tcpr-bridge reloads its state file and resumes

  # optional, in a spare terminal: watch the state change live
  ./watch-state

  # when the transfer finishes:
  ./verify                   # PASS => /tmp/out.dat is byte-identical to /tmp/in.dat

Cleanup:

  ./teardown-tcpr
  ./teardown-network

Notes:
  - tcpr-bridge persists connection metadata to
    /var/tmp/tcpr-bridge-192.0.2.1:8888.state; teardown-network removes it.
  - The clients are unmodified `nc` - the whole point is peer transparency.
  - `watch-state` uses the control tools: tcpr-get | tcpr-print.
